Track 1: Meter Running at 1:17
- Story & Context: A taxi driver finishes a twelve-hour shift but leaves the meter running while parked outside a closed print shop because the last passenger forgot a thin envelope in the back seat. The address on the envelope is only three blocks away, yet the driver hesitates: returning it means missing the final chance to go home before morning traffic. Inside the rehearsal room above the shop, Han River Relay hears the idle meter clicking through an open window and turns its uneven pulse into the song's opening rhythm. The first verse is sparse: muted gayageum harmonics, fretless bass, rim clicks, and Rhodes ninth chords. The second verse adds haegeum lines that repeatedly rise but refuse to resolve. At the bridge, the driver finally walks the envelope to the address and discovers it contains nothing valuable—only old photographs someone was afraid to lose. The chorus returns warmer, with daegeum breath, wide chorus guitar, and a bass line that stops chasing the downbeat. The emotional center is not generosity as a grand gesture; it is the cost of choosing one small responsibility after exhaustion has already won.
