Track 2: Lacquer Dust on Your Sleeve
- Story & Context: During the final week before an old furniture workshop closes, a young architect comes to measure a lacquer cabinet that will be preserved in a new development. The cabinet maker, who has worked there for forty years, is irritated by the clinical documentation and keeps wiping away the dust before measurements can be taken. The song begins as a dry jazz-funk pocket: Rhodes, kick, fretless bass, and percussive gayageum muted close to the bridge. Haegeum enters only in short two-note answers. As the two characters work, the architect notices that the cabinet dimensions do not match the drawings because the maker has been correcting tiny warps by eye for decades. The arrangement gradually loosens; janggu accents pull against the electronic snare, and the gayageum pattern shifts from sixteenth-note precision into broader ringing chords. In the final scene, the architect stops cleaning one streak of reddish lacquer dust from a dark sleeve and photographs it beside the measurements. The chorus is built around that mark: proof that preservation without the worker's touch would preserve the object but lose the knowledge.
