Track 3: Blue Hour Between Two Bridges
- Story & Context: Two former bandmates who stopped speaking years ago are separately hired for the same dawn broadcast performance on a river barge. One arrives with a haegeum; the other brings a Rhodes and assumes there has been a scheduling mistake. Neither leaves because both need the work. The producer asks for a soundcheck before sunrise. The first minute of the track contains almost no drums: electric piano voicings, distant traffic noise, and a single haegeum note that keeps bending around the major third without landing. Gayageum enters with a repeating five-note figure, while bass quietly outlines passing chords beneath it. Their musical responses become more direct than their conversation. By the middle section, the rhythm settles into 112 BPM with janggu-derived 3+3+2 accents crossing a city-pop backbeat. As blue hour turns into morning, the two players stop discussing the old argument and instead decide how to end the arrangement. They choose an unresolved suspended chord and hold it until a real boat horn enters the recording. The track closes on that accidental note, preserving reconciliation as a practical decision to continue playing rather than a verbal apology.
